---
import CaseStudyLayout from '@components/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import StatCallout from '@components/StatCallout.astro';
import LinkButton from '@components/LinkButton.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="NBA Betting Model"
  subtitle="A hybrid team + player model for NBA moneylines, spreads and totals — built as a full production pipeline and evaluated honestly against the betting market."
  category="Machine Learning"
  tools={['Python', 'LightGBM', 'FLAML', 'scikit-learn', 'SQLite', 'pdfplumber', 'nba_api', 'Streamlit', 'Plotly']}
  duration="Seasons 2019-20 to present · running daily in-season"
  ogDescription="Predicting NBA games with machine learning: point-in-time features, walk-forward validation, and a live Streamlit dashboard."
>

  <!-- OBJECTIVE -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Objective</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Can a model built from public data beat the market?</h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>
        Predict NBA moneylines, spreads and totals, then measure the results against the
        market rather than against raw win/loss records. The project is a full pipeline:
        data ingestion, point-in-time features, gradient-boosted models, and a production
        schedule that runs daily during the season.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="cs-divider" />

  <!-- DATA SOURCES -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Data Sources</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Box scores, opening and closing lines, and official injury reports</h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>
        Box scores come from <span class="cs-code-inline">nba_api</span>; opening and closing
        lines come from Sports Book Review. I also archived and parsed the official NBA
        injury report PDFs (2019-20 onward) with <span class="cs-code-inline">pdfplumber</span>.
        Everything lands in a ~1 GB SQLite database.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mt-8">
      <StatCallout value="7,936" label="Games backtested" sublabel="2019-20 to 2025-26" />
      <StatCallout value="3" label="Markets modeled" sublabel="Moneyline · spread · total" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="cs-divider" />

  <!-- METHODOLOGY -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Methodology</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Point-in-time features, tuned gradient boosting, walk-forward evaluation</h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-white/90 text-base mt-2 mb-2">Features — only what was known before tip-off</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong class="text-white/85">Team form:</strong> exponentially weighted box-score and efficiency stats (5- and 20-game spans), offense-vs-defense matchups, recent record.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white/85">Players:</strong> rolling Box Plus/Minus per player, aggregated to the roster and centered on a trailing league mean.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white/85">Schedule:</strong> rest, back-to-backs, games in the last 7 days, travel distance, time zones, altitude.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white/85">Injuries:</strong> the report as published at a fixed time on game day, with each absence weighted by player value and by the empirical chance that status actually sits.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <pre class="cs-code"># Probability a player with each report status actually plays (empirical)
Out        ≈  0%
Doubtful   ≈  2%
Questionable ≈ 57%
Probable   ≈ 93%</pre>

    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>
        Training and live inference build features with the same code, and parity tests
        match to floating-point precision — so the backtest reflects what production does.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-white/90 text-base mt-6 mb-2">Models</h3>
      <p>
        One LightGBM model each for win probability, margin and total. Feature selection
        runs in two steps: cluster correlated features, then keep only those that beat a
        shuffled-target importance baseline. Hyperparameters are tuned with FLAML, and win
        probabilities are isotonically calibrated on a time-ordered holdout.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-white/90 text-base mt-6 mb-2">Evaluation</h3>
      <p>
        Walk-forward by season: train on past seasons, test on the next. Every result is
        compared with the de-vigged market consensus. Closing line value (CLV) is the main
        measure of edge, because it is far less noisy than game results.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-white/90 text-base mt-6 mb-2">Production</h3>
      <p>
        Two scheduled jobs run in-season: an odds and injury-report snapshotter every 30
        minutes on game days, and a daily inference run at 12:15 PM ET that syncs data,
        predicts the day's games and publishes the dashboard data. GitHub Actions runs smoke
        checks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="cs-divider" />

  <!-- RESULTS -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Impact & Results</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">The model finds real signal, but the market is sharp </h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>
        The market beat the model in most seasons and every market, and the model adds
        nothing beyond the closing line. But where the model disagrees with the opening
        line, the lines move toward it — a measurable, if small, edge.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
      <table class="w-full font-mono text-sm text-left border border-white/10">
        <thead class="text-white/40 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide">
          <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
            <th class="p-3 font-normal">7,936 games</th>
            <th class="p-3 font-normal">My model</th>
            <th class="p-3 font-normal">Opening line</th>
            <th class="p-3 font-normal">Closing line</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-white/70">
          <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="p-3">Moneyline log loss</td><td class="p-3">0.644</td><td class="p-3">0.613</td><td class="p-3">0.606</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="p-3">Spread MAE (pts)</td><td class="p-3">11.06</td><td class="p-3">10.61</td><td class="p-3">10.48</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3">Total MAE (pts)</td><td class="p-3">15.65</td><td class="p-3">14.55</td><td class="p-3">14.26</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      <StatCallout value="+0.26–0.42" label="Pts of CLV on totals" sublabel="t ≈ 7–8" size="md" />
      <StatCallout value="+0.12–0.23" label="Pts of CLV on spreads" sublabel="Smaller than the ~4.5% vig" size="md" />
      <StatCallout value="21–25%" label="Of line movement explained by injuries" sublabel="Priced fully by the close" size="md" />
    </div>

    <div class="cs-prose mt-8">
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white/85">Injury news is the main thing that moves NBA lines after
        the open, and the close prices it completely.</strong> The closing line's error is
        unrelated to reported absences (t ≈ 0). No snapshot of the injury report I tested beats
        the opening line at a statistically credible level — the usable signal is timing, worth
        about +1.8 points of CLV if you act before the market moves. I now run a timestamped
        odds collector in-season to test whether that is achievable.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="cs-divider" />

  <!-- CHALLENGES -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Challenges & Tradeoffs</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Limitations and next steps</h2>
    <div class="cs-prose">
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white/85">Beating the closing line from public box scores is not
        realistic.</strong> The avenues left are timing — information before the market
        reacts — and softer markets such as player props.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white/85">A direct "line adjustment" model</strong> that predicts
        the opening line's error from injury news should extract more than bolting injury
        features onto a general model, which was a statistical null in ablation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white/85">Early-season cold start</strong> is handled with
        exponentially weighted averages that carry across seasons; backtests show the model
        doesn't fall further behind the market early. Roster- and market-based priors are a
        possible improvement.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="cs-divider" />

  <!-- LINKS -->
  <section>
    <p class="cs-section-label">Links</p>
    <h2 class="cs-section-title">Code & live dashboard</h2>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
      <LinkButton href="https://github.com/jnish23/nba-model-hybrid" label="GitHub Repo" />
      <LinkButton variant="primary" href="https://nba-model-betting-dashboard.streamlit.app/" label="Open Live Dashboard" />
    </div>
    <p class="font-mono text-xs text-white/30 mt-4">
      Dashboard: today's picks, recent performance, a strategy simulator, and team and league breakdowns.
    </p>
    <!-- TODO: add dashboard screenshots from /public/images/ (e.g. nba-dashboard.png) -->
  </section>

</CaseStudyLayout>
